 ## Background
-The concept of Style Transfer Functions (STF) for illustrative volume rendering was introduced by Stefan Bruckner and Eduard Gröller in 2007 [S1]. The paper "Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering" proposes using 2D style textures (like lit spheres) mapped via surface normals to replace traditional lighting models, allowing for non-photorealistic visualization. The method also introduces illustrative transparency to enhance the perception of 3D structure by modulating opacity based on curvature and gradient magnitude, adhering to the "100-percent-rule" of illustration [S1].
+Originating from Bruckner and Gröller's work on Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering [S1], this approach emerged to address the limitations of physical lighting in visualizing complex volumetric structures. It allows artists and scientists to apply non-photorealistic styles (e.g., cartoon shading, matcaps) directly to volume data, enhancing the perception of shape and transparency through illustrative techniques like the 100-percent-rule [S1]. The method is implemented using GPU-based ray casting, leveraging Shader Model 3.0 features for dynamic branching and texture lookups [S1].
 
